@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmt:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/traditions/> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#
# prima-materia — the mysteries of Mithras
#
# Opened at one place only, and it is a hostile outsider's report: the ladder
# of seven metal gates that Celsus adduces, preserved because Origen quotes
# him in order to attack him. The file exists because that single place is the
# only text in this graph's whole ascent material that assigns a planet to each
# of seven gates one at a time, and because it stands nine chapters before the
# ophite gate speeches in the same book, with the witness himself demanding the
# comparison.
#
# Everything else this tradition rests on — the seven grades and their
# planetary tutelage, the question-and-answer papyrus, the cave interpretation,
# the two hostile Latin reports — is named in the corpus and unread. See
# prima-materia#642.
#

pmt:Mithraic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Mysteries of Mithras"@en, "Mithras-Mysterien"@de ;
    skos:definition "The initiatory cult of the Roman empire, organised in graded degrees and practised in artificial caves, whose own transmission is almost entirely monumental and epigraphic rather than literary."@en ;
    skos:note "What was read: Origen, Contra Celsum VI.22, in the Ante-Nicene Fathers translation with the Greek of the disputed words in its notes; and Porphyry, De antro nympharum, complete, in Thomas Taylor's translation (London 1917). Neither is a Mithraic text. The first is a report about Mithraists inside a Christian refutation; the second is a Platonist reading Homer, who reaches for the cult four times because it suits his argument about the descent of souls."@en ;
    skos:note "The lopsidedness of the corpus is the first thing to say about it. This tradition left several hundred sanctuaries, reliefs and inscriptions and no book of its own; every connected account of what it taught comes from someone outside it, and mostly from someone attacking it. That is the opposite of every other tradition in this graph's ascent material, where a text survives and the material remains do not, and it is the reason the tradition can matter here as a witness independent of the heresiological channel — once the material rather than the reports is opened."@en ;
    skos:note "The name Mithras appears in this graph twice over, and the two occurrences are not the same thing. Here it is the cult. In traditions/greek-magical-papyri.ttl it is a modern title given in 1903 to PGM IV.475-829, and whether that piece is Mithraic at all is disputed in the scholarship. Nothing in this file rests on that identification."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The monuments and inscriptions of the cult in their standard corpus; the literary testimonia, among them Origen, Contra Celsum VI.22 (reporting Celsus), Porphyry, De antro nympharum, Tertullian, De corona 15, Jerome, Epistula 107.2, and Firmicus Maternus, De errore profanarum religionum 5" ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.22 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV)" ;
    dcterms:source "Porphyry, De antro nympharum (trans. Thomas Taylor, London 1917)" .

#
# The ladder, and what it is a representation of
#

pmc:MithraicKlimaxHeptapylos a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The ladder of seven gates with an eighth above it"@en, "Die Leiter mit sieben Toren und einem achten darüber"@de ;
    skos:definition "The symbol Celsus reports from the mysteries: a ladder of gates with a further gate at its top, standing for the two celestial revolutions — that of the fixed sphere and that of the planets — and for the passage of the soul through them. The first gate is of lead, the second of tin, the third of bronze, the fourth of iron, the fifth of an alloy, the sixth of silver, the seventh of gold."@en ;
    skos:note "The count of seven rests on a conjecture. The manuscript reading is a ladder of lofty gates; a seven-gated ladder is Bouherel's emendation, printed in the notes of the translation used and repeated ever since without the qualification. Seven metals are then listed, so the emendation is well motivated — but the phrase in which the number is usually quoted is not the transmitted one, and every argument that counts this ladder as a seven-station ascent is counting the metals, not reading the word."@en ;
    skos:note "It is a representation and not an instruction. The witness says what the symbol shows; he does not say that anyone climbs it, what is said at a gate, or what happens to a soul that fails at one. Beside the ophite speeches, which give the words, this is the same architecture with the operative half missing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mithraic ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.22 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV)" .

pmc:MithraicPylaiPlanetarum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Each gate assigned to one planet, with a reason from its metal"@en, "Jedes Tor einem Planeten zugeordnet, mit einer Begründung aus seinem Metall"@de ;
    skos:definition "The assignment Celsus reports gate by gate: the leaden gate to Saturn for the slowness of that star, the tin one to Venus for brightness and softness, the bronze one to Jupiter as firm-based and solid, the iron one to Mercury because iron and Mercury alike endure every work, make money and are laborious, the alloyed one to Mars because a mixture is uneven and varied, the silver one to the Moon and the golden one to the Sun, after the colours of the two."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the only place in the whole ascent material of this graph where a late antique witness distributes planets over seven gates one at a time. The ophite diagram does not: it counts by gates and realms, and the single heavenly body named anywhere in it is Phainon, in sympathy with the lion-shaped ruler of the other, differently named hebdomad. Irenaeus I.30.9 equates the hebdomad with the planets collectively and never pairs a name with a star. Every modern table that seats Ialdabaoth on Saturn is therefore filling one witness from another, and this is the nearest candidate for where the filling came from — nine chapters earlier in the same book, in a passage whose author expressly invites the comparison of the two diagrams. See prima-materia#595 and prima-materia#641."@en ;
    skos:note "The order is the test, not the resemblance. Saturn, Venus, Jupiter, Mercury, Mars, Moon, Sun is neither the received planetary sequence by distance nor the order in which the ophite speaker meets his gates. A transfer from here to there would have had to change the order while keeping the idea, which is a stronger claim than a borrowing and is not made by anyone yet."@en ;
    skos:note "Celsus gave the reason for the sequence and the witness withheld it. Origen reports that he went on to examine why the stars stand in this order, that musical reasons out of Persian theology were adduced, and that a second musical explanation followed — and then declines to quote any of it as beneath him. What is lost is exactly the part that would say whether the order is arbitrary, harmonic or observational. The project's own criterion names this gate: an order held back is a gate, and here it was held back by the transmitter rather than by the tradition."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mithraic ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.22 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV)" .

pmc:MithraicComparatioImposita a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The comparison of the two diagrams is demanded by the witness"@en, "Der Vergleich der beiden Diagramme wird vom Zeugen selbst verlangt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The frame in which both accounts reach us: Celsus adduces the Persian mysteries in order to say that whoever examines the Christian mysteries alongside them, and unveils the rites of the Christians, will see the difference; Origen answers that the comparison was chosen for polemical use, that Eleusis, Aegina, Egypt, Cappadocia, Thrace and Rome would have served as well, and that Celsus never says which sect uses the diagram he describes."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as a node because it decides how much the two accounts can be worth to each other. They are not two independent reports that happen to agree; they are one author's pairing, preserved by a second author who thought the pairing tendentious. Any correspondence between them is therefore at most a second occurrence in one channel, never a second witness — the same rule that keeps the star-formula of PGM IV apart from the ophite speeches."@en ;
    skos:note "Origen's complaint carries a fact worth keeping: Celsus, who names sects wherever he can, does not name the one whose diagram he has. The ophite attribution of that diagram is not in the witness."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mithraic ;
    dcterms:source "Origen, Contra Celsum VI.22 (trans. F. Crombie, Ante-Nicene Fathers IV)" .

#
# The cave, and what the second witness saw in it
#

pmc:MithraicSpelaeumImagoMundi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The cave is an image of the world, and its furnishings are its regions"@en, "Die Höhle ist ein Abbild der Welt, und ihre Ausstattung sind deren Gegenden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The report Porphyry takes from Eubulus: that Zoroaster first consecrated to Mithras a naturally formed cave in the mountains of Persia, flowering and with springs, as a likeness of the world Mithras had made; and that the things placed in the cave were arranged at commensurate intervals, so as to be symbols of the elements and regions of that world. Wherever the god was known, Porphyry adds, he was approached in a cave, and the initiate of the Persians is admitted in a place they call a cavern, which signifies the descent of the soul into the sublunary and its return."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the only statement in the graph that a ritual space is laid out to scale. It does not merely say that the cave means the world; it says the interval between two things in it corresponds to the interval between two things in the world. A claim of that shape is checkable against a surviving sanctuary in a way that an allegory is not, and the check has not been made here — the seven semicircles of the mithraeum at Ostia are the obvious place to make it, and they are unread. See prima-materia#642."@en ;
    skos:note "The chain of hands is three long and the middle link is lost. Porphyry reports Eubulus, whose work on Mithras does not survive; Eubulus reports the Persians. The graph holds the outermost link only, and every statement here about what Zoroaster did is at that distance."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mithraic ;
    dcterms:source "Porphyry, De antro nympharum, on the cave consecrated by Zoroaster (trans. Thomas Taylor, London 1917, section 2 of that edition's division)" .

pmc:MithraicLeonticaMelle a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Honey poured on the hands of those initiated in the rites of the Lion"@en, "Honig über die Hände derer, die in die Löwenweihe aufgenommen werden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The purification Porphyry reports from within the cult: those initiated into the Leontic rites have honey poured over their hands in place of water, and are charged by the initiator to keep their hands clean of everything harmful, injurious and abominable; and their tongue is likewise purified of evil with honey. Other initiators into the same mysteries use fire instead, as a purifying agent."@en ;
    skos:note "A named grade, a named substance, a stated substitution and a stated charge, all in one sentence — and the charge is a condition laid on the initiate rather than a promise made to him. That is rarer in this graph than any vision. What the passage does not say is what follows if the charge is broken."@en ;
    skos:note "The substitution is the operative part. Water is what hands are washed with; honey is what preserves against decay, which is the property Porphyry has spent the surrounding pages establishing. The gesture therefore says that these hands are to be kept from corruption, not merely cleaned, and the alternative purification by fire says the same thing with the other means."@en ;
    skos:note "Two further Mithraic details stand in the same passage and are held here rather than as separate nodes: the Persians offer honey to the guardian of fruits as a symbol of a preserving and warding power, and a bowl stands beside Mithras in place of a spring."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mithraic ;
    dcterms:source "Porphyry, De antro nympharum, on honey in the Leontic rites (trans. Thomas Taylor, London 1917, section 7 of that edition's division)" .

pmc:MithraicSedesAequinoctialis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Mithras is seated at the equinox, between the two gates"@en, "Mithras sitzt am Äquinoktium, zwischen den beiden Toren"@de ;
    skos:definition "The position Porphyry assigns him: a place beside the equinoctial circle as his proper seat, holding the sword of Aries, a martial sign, and borne on the Bull, which is the sign of Venus, because Mithras like the Bull is demiurge and lord of generation; he stands with the northern parts on his right hand and the southern on his left."@en ;
    skos:note "The two gates in this treatise are Cancer for the descent of souls and Capricorn for their ascent, and they are the doctrine of the theologians Porphyry follows — Numenius and Cronius — not a Mithraic teaching. What is Mithraic in the passage is only the seat: the god is placed at the crossing between the two, that is, at neither gate but at the point where descent and ascent are equal."@en ;
    skos:note "Held apart from the ladder of Contra Celsum VI.22 on purpose. That account has seven gates in a line and a soul that traverses them; this one has two gates at the solstices and a god who does not traverse anything. They are two cosmologies for one cult, reported by two outsiders a generation apart, and the graph does not reconcile them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mithraic ;
    dcterms:source "Porphyry, De antro nympharum, on the seat of Mithras at the equinoctial circle (trans. Thomas Taylor, London 1917, section 11 of that edition's division)" .
